3. Semantic Executability

Pantheon recognizes multiple forms of executability.

Class 0 — Fossil

Interesting historical material.

No present capability claim.

Class 1 — Evocative

The artifact suggests a potentially useful mechanism but does not yet constrain reconstruction sufficiently.

It makes the interpreter think.

It does not yet reliably tell the interpreter how to think.

Class 2 — Reconstructable

A competent interpreter can recover:

* the intended transformation,
* relevant inputs,
* expected outputs,
* important invariants,
* approximate stopping conditions.

Class 3 — Exercisable

The reconstructed operator can actually be applied to a target.

O(X)\rightarrow Y

Class 4 — Demonstrated

Exercising the operator produces an observable useful delta.

Fitness(Y)>Fitness(X)

under some stated objective.

Class 5 — Portable

Independent interpreters reconstruct sufficiently equivalent operator behavior.

R_{GPT}(O)
\approx
R_{Claude}(O)
\approx
R_{Human}(O)

The implementations may differ.

The important invariants survive.

Class 6 — Sealed

The mechanism has become sufficiently stable, useful, reconstructable, and testable to enter Pantheon’s callable capability library.

Class 7 — Native

The capability additionally possesses conventional software, tooling, automation, or another direct execution substrate.

Native executability is therefore one endpoint, not Pantheon’s definition of capability.

